WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:26.919
Welcome to the first live

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.119
taping of the recent interview

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.199
podcast.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.048
I am your host, Nick Gillespie.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:33.220
Thank you for being here.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.929
And our our guest

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:40.639
tonight is one of the most

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.309
provocative, thoughtful, heterodox,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.259
courageous writers at work today

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:46.759
and this is her fourth glass of

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.709
wine, so hopefully she's also

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.629
one of the drunks.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.119
She is certainly probably

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.069
the only writer living dead

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.229
or who will ever be conceived or

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.209
created in a laboratory who

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:00.919
has been compared both to Norman

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.630
Mailer and Roseanne Barr.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.689
Laura Kipnis is

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.189
the author of a shelf full of books

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.439
including Ecstasy, Unlimited Against

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.629
Love, Unwanted Advances

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.069
and the main subject of tonight's

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:13.849
conversation.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:15.379
Love in a Time.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.989
Love in the Time of Contagion.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.519
She taught film and journalism at

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.469
Northwestern and a number of other

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.849
places, and she's been published

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.439
everywhere The New York Times,

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.359
Harper's The Atlantic Slate,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.949
and Even Reason.

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.749
Despite being a lapsed Marxist

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.389
feminist, by her own admission,

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.069
what's always made her work stand

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:36.109
out to me and I think I

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.729
this is a widely shared

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.769
appreciation, is an attention

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.659
to the telling detail and a pursuit

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:43.969
of the truth even and especially

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.009
when it upsets settled ideological

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.839
doctrine and dogma, and

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.429
an intellectual and emotional

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.379
curiosity that pulls the reader

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.449
into the dark forest of sexuality,

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:55.159
gender and longing.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.019
Maybe most importantly, for the

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.489
current moment, she's also a

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.019
champion of free speech and free

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.879
expression in a world that seems

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.439
kind of upset with that.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.389
So, Laura Kipnis,

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.249
thanks for talking to RESA.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.359
Thank you.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.129
Was you? You pull out a little bit

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:10.579
of her.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.359
Thank you, Nick.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.649
Thank you, everyone.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.629
So, Laura, tell

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.579
Start, what's the

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.529
elevator pitch for love in the time

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:19.309
of contagion?

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.239
And what do you what do you hope

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.219
people walk away from the book

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:23.149
thinking?

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:25.039
Well, so I

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.079
had written this book Against Love,

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.149
a polemic a couple of decades

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.549
ago, which was this kind of anti

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.679
domestic tract

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.379
about the prison and premature.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:38.749
Is this sort of echoing Tell me if,

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:40.159
yeah, no, it's OK.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.529
It's about like premature

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.779
cemetery of domestic couple Tomb

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.149
Coupledom and then found myself

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.309
ensconced in domestic coupledom

00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:50.579
during the lockdown.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.239
So is the greater pickup line up.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:54.649
You put it out on the street and

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.389
somebody picked it up?

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:57.919
Yes, something like that.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.629
Yeah. I mean, you know, somebody

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:00.439
wanted.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.389
They were going to make you

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.299
part of domestic bliss.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:06.529
Something like that happen.

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:07.530
It's true.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.129
But I mean, I'm also very interested

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:12.499
in the ways that like as a lapsed

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.419
Marxist feminist or possibly

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.159
residual or something.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.289
I mean, you know, I quote Marx

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.389
in the book in the sense that we

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.889
don't get to make history under

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:23.479
circumstances that we choose.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.989
And I say the same thing about love,

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.479
you know, we don't get to love under

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.829
the circumstances we choose, you

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:29.659
know, we're loving in historical

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.609
time and contending with

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:33.229
all the things that are dumped on US

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:35.059
historical forces and

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.889
this virus, you know, which was in

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:38.349
a lot of ways, an historical force

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.379
as well.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.389
So in the same way that it sort

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.399
of held this microscope up to

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.469
the country and exposed all of the

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.999
inequities and exposed all these

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:47.929
things that were sort of there all

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:49.639
along that nobody wanted to look at.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.049
I think it did the same thing for

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:53.329
love and our love lives.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.819
So I talk about my own situation

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.649
in some detail and

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.569
also talked to other people

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.609
about what they were dealing with.

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.589
And you know what,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.809
what people learned and

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.699
didn't learn and discovered

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.949
and exposed and all of that.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:11.329
You know, one of the things that I

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.589
like about the book and your writing

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:13.999
in general, too, is that it is

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:14.929
personal.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.148
And I don't know if it's

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:18.768
maybe it's confessional, but it's

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:20.749
never exhibitionist

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:21.828
or narcissistic.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:23.749
So I don't know how you do that,

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.609
but you talk about things in

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.949
a way that is just

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:29.869
revelatory rather than

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.739
kind of like, Why am I reading this?

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:32.740
Thank you.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.449
Early on in the book, you talk about

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.669
how in passing

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.129
kind of talk about how COVID

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.029
is, you know,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.209
it echoes AIDS,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:45.470
not in

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:47.779
in various ways.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.699
And do you think or and

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.289
you say you wonder whether or not

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:52.909
COVID will have the same kind of

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:54.859
long tail effect on

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.679
everything that AIDS had?

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.229
What is the what's the crux

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.119
of the impact of AIDS, do you

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.249
think? And you know, have you

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:05.629
since writing the book, have you

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.639
figured out like, yeah, COVID is

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.349
actually going to be haunting us for

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:10.820
a long time? Or maybe not.

